### What is Kafka and when do you choose it?

Kafka is a distributed commit log where producers append events to partitioned topics. The defining property is that Kafka retains messages based on a time or size policy, not based on whether they were consumed. Multiple independent consumer groups can read the same events, and a consumer can rewind its offset to re-read old events. This makes Kafka ideal for event streaming, event sourcing, and any scenario where multiple systems need the same data.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh's transfer event is published once to the `transfer-events` topic, and the fraud service, audit service, and notification service each consume it independently. If the fraud team needs to replay last week's events to retrain a model, they simply seek back to an earlier offset.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferEventProducer {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public TransferEventProducer(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransfer(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        String event = "{\"accountId\":\"" + accountId
            + "\",\"amount\":" + amount + "}";
        kafkaTemplate.send("transfer-events", accountId, event);
        System.out.println("Published transfer event for: " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Published transfer event for: SAV2001
-> Fraud service reads it (fraud-group)
-> Audit service reads it (audit-group)
-> Notification service reads it (notification-group)
```

### What is AWS SQS and when do you choose it?

SQS is a pull-based, fully managed queue. A message lives in the queue until a consumer deletes it or the retention window (up to 14 days) expires. When a consumer reads a message, it becomes invisible for a visibility timeout; if the consumer crashes before deleting it, the message reappears for another worker. SQS is ideal when exactly one worker should perform a background job, and you want zero infrastructure to manage.

At National Bank of India, when a monthly statement needs to be generated as a PDF, the system puts a job message into an SQS queue. One worker picks it up, generates the PDF, and deletes the message. No Kafka infrastructure needed for this simple background task.

```java
package com.bank.sqs;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sqs.SqsClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sqs.model.SendMessageRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sqs.model.ReceiveMessageRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sqs.model.DeleteMessageRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sqs.model.Message;
import java.util.List;

public class StatementJobQueue {

    private final SqsClient sqsClient;
    private final String queueUrl;

    public StatementJobQueue(SqsClient sqsClient, String queueUrl) {
        this.sqsClient = sqsClient;
        this.queueUrl = queueUrl;
    }

    public void enqueueStatementJob(String accountId, String month) {
        String body = "{\"accountId\":\"" + accountId
            + "\",\"month\":\"" + month + "\"}";
        sqsClient.sendMessage(SendMessageRequest.builder()
            .queueUrl(queueUrl).messageBody(body).build());
        System.out.println("Enqueued statement job: " + accountId);
    }

    public void processJobs() {
        List<Message> messages = sqsClient.receiveMessage(
            ReceiveMessageRequest.builder()
                .queueUrl(queueUrl).maxNumberOfMessages(5).build()
        ).messages();

        for (Message msg : messages) {
            System.out.println("Generating statement: " + msg.body());
            // Generate PDF...
            sqsClient.deleteMessage(DeleteMessageRequest.builder()
                .queueUrl(queueUrl)
                .receiptHandle(msg.receiptHandle()).build());
            System.out.println("Job completed and deleted");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Enqueued statement job: SAV2001
Generating statement: {"accountId":"SAV2001","month":"2025-01"}
Job completed and deleted
```

### What is RabbitMQ and when do you choose it?

RabbitMQ is a smart broker implementing the AMQP protocol. Producers publish to an exchange, not directly to a queue. The exchange uses a routing key and bindings to decide which queue receives the message. A direct exchange matches the routing key exactly, a topic exchange matches wildcard patterns like `loan.*.mumbai`, and a fanout exchange broadcasts to all bound queues. RabbitMQ is excellent for complex routing logic that changes frequently.

At National Bank of India, loan applications need routing based on region and loan type. A topic exchange with routing keys like `loan.home.mumbai` routes the application to the Mumbai home loan desk, while `loan.personal.delhi` goes to the Delhi personal loan desk.

```java
package com.bank.rabbitmq;

import org.springframework.amqp.rabbit.core.RabbitTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class LoanApplicationRouter {

    private final RabbitTemplate rabbitTemplate;

    public LoanApplicationRouter(RabbitTemplate rabbitTemplate) {
        this.rabbitTemplate = rabbitTemplate;
    }

    public void routeLoanApplication(String loanType, String region,
                                     String applicationJson) {
        String routingKey = "loan." + loanType + "." + region;
        rabbitTemplate.convertAndSend("loan-exchange", routingKey,
            applicationJson);
        System.out.println("Routed loan application: " + routingKey);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Routed loan application: loan.home.mumbai
-> Delivered to mumbai-home-loan-queue (bound to loan.home.mumbai)

Routed loan application: loan.personal.delhi
-> Delivered to delhi-personal-loan-queue (bound to loan.personal.delhi)
```

### How are Kafka, SQS, and RabbitMQ different? (comparison table)

The differences are rooted in the core design philosophy. Kafka is a log, SQS is a queue, and RabbitMQ is a router.

| Feature | Kafka | AWS SQS | RabbitMQ |
|---|---|---|---|
| Core model | Append-only event log | Managed point-to-point queue | Smart broker with exchanges |
| Message retention | Time/size based, independent of consumption | Until consumed or 14 days max | Until consumed and acknowledged |
| Multiple consumers | Yes, via consumer groups (each gets all) | No, one consumer per message | Yes, via fanout exchange |
| Replay | Yes, seek to any offset | No, message deleted after ack | No, message removed after ack |
| Ordering | Per partition (by key) | Best-effort (Standard), strict (FIFO) | Per queue |
| Routing | Topic + key-based partitioning | None (simple queue) | Exchanges, routing keys, bindings |
| Throughput | Millions of events/second | Thousands/second (scales with SQS) | Tens of thousands/second |
| Operations | Self-managed cluster | Zero (fully managed by AWS) | Self-managed broker |
| Delivery | At-least-once, exactly-once with transactions | At-least-once (Standard), exactly-once (FIFO) | At-least-once, at-most-once |
| Best for | Event streaming, event sourcing, CDC | Background jobs, spike buffering | Complex routing, request-reply |

```java
package com.bank.comparison;

public class MessagingDecisionMatrix {

    public static String chooseSystem(boolean needsReplay,
                                       boolean needsRouting,
                                       boolean zeroOps) {
        if (needsReplay) {
            return "Kafka: durable log with replay capability";
        }
        if (needsRouting) {
            return "RabbitMQ: exchange-based routing";
        }
        if (zeroOps) {
            return "SQS: fully managed, zero infrastructure";
        }
        return "Kafka: default for event-driven architecture";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(chooseSystem(true, false, false));
        System.out.println(chooseSystem(false, true, false));
        System.out.println(chooseSystem(false, false, true));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka: durable log with replay capability
RabbitMQ: exchange-based routing
SQS: fully managed, zero infrastructure
```

### What are the common mistakes when choosing a messaging system?

The most common mistake is using one system for everything when a different system would be a better fit. Another mistake is choosing Kafka for simple background jobs where SQS would be simpler and cheaper.

Wrong approach: using Kafka for a simple email notification job.

```java
package com.bank.messaging;

// WRONG: Using Kafka for a simple one-off background job.
// Kafka is overkill here. You maintain a Kafka cluster, configure
// topics, partitions, consumer groups, and retention for a task
// that just needs one worker to send one email.
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class WrongEmailNotifier {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "email-jobs", groupId = "email-group")
    public void sendEmail(String payload) {
        // Simple email sending - does not need replay, multiple consumers,
        // or durable event history
        System.out.println("Sending email: " + payload);
    }
}
```

Correct approach: using SQS for the simple job, Kafka for the event stream.

```java
package com.bank.messaging;

// CORRECT: SQS for simple background jobs, Kafka for event streams.
// SQS is fully managed, no cluster to maintain, perfect for
// one-worker-per-job patterns.
public class CorrectSystemChoice {

    // Use Kafka for the event that triggered the notification
    // kafkaTemplate.send("transfer-completed", accountId, event);

    // Use SQS for the actual email sending job
    // sqsClient.sendMessage(queueUrl, emailPayload);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Kafka: publish transfer-completed event");
        System.out.println("  -> Fraud service reads event (Kafka consumer)");
        System.out.println("  -> Audit service reads event (Kafka consumer)");
        System.out.println("SQS: enqueue email job");
        System.out.println("  -> One worker sends email and deletes job");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka: publish transfer-completed event
  -> Fraud service reads event (Kafka consumer)
  -> Audit service reads event (Kafka consumer)
SQS: enqueue email job
  -> One worker sends email and deletes job
```

### What about using all three together in a banking system?

A mature banking platform at National Bank of India uses all three systems, each for the workload it handles best. Kafka is the event backbone for all financial events. SQS handles background jobs like report generation and PDF creation. RabbitMQ handles legacy routing-heavy integrations where messages need to reach specific desks based on complex rules.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class MessagingArchitectureOverview {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("National Bank of India Messaging Architecture");
        System.out.println("============================================");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka (Event Backbone):");
        System.out.println("  - transfer-events: all money movements");
        System.out.println("  - trade-events: stock trading activity");
        System.out.println("  - audit-events: compliance logging");
        System.out.println("  Consumers: fraud, audit, notification, analytics");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("SQS (Background Jobs):");
        System.out.println("  - statement-generation-queue: monthly PDFs");
        System.out.println("  - report-queue: regulatory reports");
        System.out.println("  - email-queue: customer notifications");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("RabbitMQ (Routing):");
        System.out.println("  - loan-exchange: route by type and region");
        System.out.println("  - support-exchange: route by priority and dept");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
National Bank of India Messaging Architecture
============================================

Kafka (Event Backbone):
  - transfer-events: all money movements
  - trade-events: stock trading activity
  - audit-events: compliance logging
  Consumers: fraud, audit, notification, analytics

SQS (Background Jobs):
  - statement-generation-queue: monthly PDFs
  - report-queue: regulatory reports
  - email-queue: customer notifications

RabbitMQ (Routing):
  - loan-exchange: route by type and region
  - support-exchange: route by priority and dept
```

### What about throughput and scalability differences?

Kafka handles millions of events per second because it appends to a sequential log on disk and batches writes. SQS scales automatically but typically handles thousands of messages per second per queue. RabbitMQ handles tens of thousands per second but is limited by single-node memory and the complexity of exchange routing.

```java
package com.bank.benchmark;

public class ThroughputComparison {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Throughput Comparison (approximate):");
        System.out.println("Kafka:    1,000,000+ events/sec (batched, sequential I/O)");
        System.out.println("SQS:      3,000 msg/sec (standard), 300 msg/sec (FIFO)");
        System.out.println("RabbitMQ: 20,000-50,000 msg/sec (depends on routing)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Banking recommendation:");
        System.out.println("  High-volume trade feed     -> Kafka");
        System.out.println("  Statement generation jobs  -> SQS");
        System.out.println("  Loan routing to branches   -> RabbitMQ");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Throughput Comparison (approximate):
Kafka:    1,000,000+ events/sec (batched, sequential I/O)
SQS:      3,000 msg/sec (standard), 300 msg/sec (FIFO)
RabbitMQ: 20,000-50,000 msg/sec (depends on routing)

Banking recommendation:
  High-volume trade feed     -> Kafka
  Statement generation jobs  -> SQS
  Loan routing to branches   -> RabbitMQ
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask why you would not just use Kafka for everything. The answer is that Kafka adds operational complexity: you manage a broker cluster, configure partitions, manage consumer groups, and handle retention. For a simple background job, SQS gives you the same reliability with zero ops. They also ask when RabbitMQ beats Kafka. The answer is complex routing: RabbitMQ's exchange-binding model handles patterns like `loan.*.mumbai` natively, while Kafka would require multiple topics or application-level filtering.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class InterviewFollowUp {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Q: Why not use Kafka for everything?");
        System.out.println("A: Kafka needs a cluster, partitions, consumer groups.");
        System.out.println("   SQS is zero-ops for simple jobs.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Q: When does RabbitMQ beat Kafka?");
        System.out.println("A: Complex routing with exchanges and wildcard keys.");
        System.out.println("   Kafka requires app-level filtering for the same.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Q: Can you use Kafka and SQS together?");
        System.out.println("A: Yes. Kafka for events, SQS for background jobs.");
        System.out.println("   A Kafka consumer can enqueue SQS jobs.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Q: Why not use Kafka for everything?
A: Kafka needs a cluster, partitions, consumer groups.
   SQS is zero-ops for simple jobs.

Q: When does RabbitMQ beat Kafka?
A: Complex routing with exchanges and wildcard keys.
   Kafka requires app-level filtering for the same.

Q: Can you use Kafka and SQS together?
A: Yes. Kafka for events, SQS for background jobs.
   A Kafka consumer can enqueue SQS jobs.
```
